  {% set yesimBest = providerB.codes | findCode(providerB.primaryCode) %}

  <div class="content-callout">
    <h2>The Short Answer</h2>
    <p>
      Pick <strong>Airalo</strong> if you want a fixed, per-gigabyte plan for a trip you can already size, usually at a lower sticker price. Pick <strong>Yesim</strong> if you fly often and would rather keep one dollar balance that carries over between trips, and you value a 30-day refund window on unused plans. Neither is the cheapest option on the market, so check the final price for your destination before you buy.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>The Core Difference: A Fixed Plan or a Rolling Balance</h2>
  <p>
    Airalo sells data by the gigabyte. You choose a country, region or global plan with a set amount of data and a validity window (typically 7 to 30 days once activated). The eSIM itself does not expire before you activate it, so you can buy ahead of a trip. When the window or the data runs out, the plan is done.
  </p>
  <p>
    Yesim's "Pay &amp; Fly" works more like a prepaid balance. You buy data in dollars rather than gigabytes, priced from $1.10/GB on Yesim's own page, and unused balance carries over to your next trip instead of expiring when you land back home. Yesim also sells ordinary day-based and per-GB country plans, so the balance model is optional.
  </p>

