      <li><strong>Yesim:</strong> {{ providerB.primaryCode }}, {{ yesimBest.discount | lower }}. It is a public code, not one of ours. <a href="/yesim/">Full code list &rarr;</a></li>
    </ul>
    <p style="margin-top:.75rem;margin-bottom:0;color:#63553F;">Both codes are aimed at first-time buyers. A percentage off a small per-GB plan saves less in dollars than the same percentage off a bigger bundle, so compare the price after the code, not the sticker price.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>Is Airalo or Yesim the Right Pick</h2>
  <div class="decision-grid">
    <div class="decision-card decision-card--primary">
      <h3>Go with Airalo if&hellip;</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>You know roughly how much data a single trip needs</li>
        <li>You are comfortable checking a data meter mid-trip</li>
        <li>You want a fixed price per GB and a clear cap</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="decision-card decision-card--secondary">
      <h3>Go with Yesim if&hellip;</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>You travel often and want one balance you top up trip after trip</li>
        <li>You want the option of a refund if you buy ahead and the plan goes unused</li>
        <li>You would rather pay in dollars for data used than track a fixed allowance</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>Two Things Worth Knowing Before You Decide</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Yesim is the pricier of the two on day-based plans.</strong> Yesim sits in the expensive bracket on this site, and you are paying for the refund window and the balance model more than for the lowest rate per GB. If price per GB is your only concern, <a href="/airhub/">Airhub</a> or <a href="/holafly/">Holafly</a> are usually cheaper places to start.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Airalo's "unlimited" has a daily ceiling.</strong> Regional unlimited plans run at full speed for the first 3GB each day, then throttle to 1Mbps until the allowance resets at midnight. If you want a single fixed price for a month of heavy use, see our <a href="/compare/holafly-vs-airalo/">Holafly vs Airalo</a> comparison.
  </p>
  <p>
    For device setup and eSIM basics that apply to both providers, see our <a href="/compatible-devices/">eSIM compatible devices</a> guide.
  </p>

  <h2>Airalo vs Yesim FAQ</h2>
  <div class="content-faq">
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Which is cheaper, Airalo or Yesim</h3>
      <p>For a single trip, Airalo's fixed per-GB plans are usually the cheaper of the two on sticker price. Yesim sits in the expensive bracket for day-based plans, though its Pay &amp; Fly balance starts at $1.10/GB. Compare the price for your exact country after the code.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Does Airalo or Yesim data expire</h3>
      <p>Airalo's eSIM does not expire before activation, but once activated the plan's validity window (typically 7 to 30 days) starts counting down. Yesim says unused Pay &amp; Fly balance carries over to your next trip.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Can I get a refund on either one</h3>
      <p>Yesim advertises a 30-day money-back guarantee on unused plans. We did not find a comparable refund promise for Airalo, so read its terms before buying.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Does Airalo or Yesim have a discount code</h3>
      <p>Both do. Airalo: {{ providerA.primaryCode }}, {{ airaloBest.discount | lower }}. Yesim: {{ providerB.primaryCode }}, {{ yesimBest.discount | lower }}. Both are aimed at first-time buyers.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

